  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/0912.3674v3</id>
    <title>"Nonmagnetic" Dimer Mott insulator induced by the coupling of spin with quantum electric dipoles</title>
    <updated>2010-08-26T16:47:56Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.3674v3" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/0912.3674v3" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present an effective dipolar-spin model based on the strong coupling analysis, which may explain the possible origin of the "spin liquid Mott insulator". The issue is related to the dimer Mott insulator reminiscent of the organic triangular lattice system, $κ$-ET$_2$Cu$_2$(CN)$_3$, whose gapless spin liquid state had been discussed in the context of geometrical frustration of exchange coupling, $J$, between spins on dimer orbitals. It turns out that another degrees of freedom within the insulator, {\it quantum electric-dipoles} on dimers, interacts with each other by the Coulomb interaction and brings about a significant suppression of $J$ through the dipolar-spin coupling.</summary>
    <category term="cond-mat.str-el" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2009-12-18T15:40:42Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>4pages 2figures</arxiv:comment>
